I have setup Moodle on AWS using ELB, EC2, RDS, EFS The setup is pretty straight forward. A number of EC2 instances running Apache or Nginx A classic Load Balancer to register the EC2 instances Aurora RDS database (here you might have a problem installing Moodle 3.1 and up MDL-58931 ) MariaDB would be better solution Elastic File System (EFS) that is mounted to all EC2 instances so that you can share the Moodle Core code and moodledata The problems: One huge problem is performance with this setup. Moodle was running slow, and by slow I mean 40-50 sec loading. After some test we concluded that the problem was that EFS is (for some reason) is very bad for caching. We still didn't find the best solution for this but nevertheless our cluster runs ok so far, using the methods below Use Amazon ElasticCache for Application cache. Problem: for some reason Moodle loses connectivity and automatically reverts back to Default Application Caching, making Moodle slow again. If you go to Cache Plugin and reset the Cache Store Moodle comes backs to normal or Use an EC2 instance as a memcached server. Problem: The same as above, but it rarely happens Use an EC2 instance as an NFS server for caches 
