Thanks for sharing, Michael! I've thought about this sort of thing in the past, but the concept scares me a little. I know how far behind mysql replication can get and I worry about a scenario where we get some information from an out of date slave, then write with that information back, loosing info in the middle. e.g., this contrived example: 
```text
$grade = $DB->get_record('grade', array('userid' => $user->id));
$grade->value = $grade->value + 1;
$DB->update_record('grade', $grade);
```
 Really, these sort of operations should be wrapped in a database transaction (and the driver would need to be able deal with that too), but at the moment we can't rely on transactions in Moodle core and they are used very sparingly, so could not be relied upon. Have you tried out a mysql-side solution like http://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/5.1/en/mysql-proxy.html ? I wouldn't be surprised to find someone in the community who has, or a pg solution like pgpool or pgbouncer?

It's a nice idea to start sending all READs to master as soon as any WRITE is done - this covers scenario where following SQL queries depend on the data we've changed. Dan is showing another problem (the other way around) - we first READ and then, based on it perform UPDATE: 
```text
$grade = $DB->get_record('grade', array('userid' => $user->id));
$grade->value = $grade->value + 1;
$DB->update_record('grade', $grade);
```
 The above will not work well if we read stale data from SLAVE and then write to MASTER. The driver would need to support transactions - if transaction is used (used explicitly in Moodle code), it would need to be fully executed on master only. So the above code would need to be wrapped with start_delegated_transaction() and allow_commit() calls.

